    "body": "Vior Gold President and CEO Mathieu Savard (left) chats with The Miner’s Western Editor, Henry Lazenby.\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 20, 2026\nVior Gold (TSXV: VIO; US-OTC: VIORF) plans to drill 30,000 metres at its Ligneris gold project in Quebec in 2027 to test gaps in the South zone, president and CEO Mathieu Savard says. At Xplor in early November, Savard said Vior still had about 2,000 metres to drill to reach this year’s 30,000-metre target.\nAt Ligneris, about 600 km northwest of Quebec City and 55 km northwest of Amos, Vior has traced the South zone for more than 1.3 km along strike and intersected gold 845 metres below surface. The company has yet to publish a resource. Savard expects Ligneris to account for about two-thirds of Vior’s 2027 budget.\n“We’re still at the early discovery days at Ligneris, so it’s going to take still a while before we can talk about ounces, literally,” Savard told The Northern Miner at Xplor in Montreal. “But we can already see space to drill there and there’s really good indication that we could build ounces fast there.”\nSavard wants to find 3 – 5 million oz. to attract a major producer. Vior has the money for next year’s program and wants at least three rigs at Ligneris this winter, when crews can reach some of the harder-to-drill sites.\nWatch the full video below:\nPrint\nTAG: Video\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Vior Gold\nRepublish this article",
